 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Hottest month ever recorded 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Reproduced from NOAA . Chart: Axios Visuals July was the hottest month on record for the contiguous U.S., Alex Fitzpatrick reports from new federal data dating back to 1895. 

 Last month's average temperature was 76.89°F, topping the 76.77°F mark set in July 1936. That's 3.3°F warmer than the 1901-2000 average. 🌡️ NOAA said in its monthly report : "Above-average temperatures covered the entire [contiguous U.S.], with all states ranking in the warmest third of their July temperature records and none ranking near or below average."

 July brought a series of "heat domes" across the country that trapped warm air beneath them, fueling several extreme heat events. Researchers have found that climate change is making extreme heat events more common and more intense — but they're also linked to phenomena and patterns like El Niño. Go deeper . Boats stranded in a Serbian marina along the Danube River on Aug. 7. Photo: Andrej Isakovic/AFP via Getty Images 📉 Meanwhile: Water levels along Europe's Danube River are at record lows after prolonged heat and drought.

 The phenomenon has exposed the remains of an ancient Roman-era bridge and dozens of German World War II warships. 🥵 What's next: Europe is bracing for yet another heat wave after a summer already marked by record-setting highs.

 Samantha Burgess, strategic lead for climate at the European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts, tells AP: "This is a clear example of how climate change is intensifying heat extremes, with heat and drought increasingly reinforcing one another." Read more . 
 
